---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import ToneGeneratorTool from '../../components/ToneGeneratorTool.astro';
import NewToolRelatedTests from '../../components/NewToolRelatedTests.astro';
import FAQ from '../../components/FAQ.astro';
import JsonLd from '../../components/seo/JsonLd.astro';
import { absoluteUrl } from '../../config';

const description = 'Play a Web Audio oscillator in this browser. Pick a frequency from 1 to 20000 Hz and sine, square, sawtooth, or triangle. This is playback, not a hearing test.';
const faq = [
  { question: 'Does this page test hearing?', answer: 'No. Play starts one oscillator in this browser and Stop ends it. The page does not produce a hearing score, an audiogram, or a pass or fail. A frequency you can hear, and a frequency you cannot hear, are both outside what this page records.' },
  { question: 'Can this page match tinnitus or measure frequency response?', answer: 'No. The page does not match tinnitus, and it does not measure frequency response of headphones, speakers, a room, or a sound card. It does not open the microphone, so it cannot compare the oscillator with a sound in the air.' },
  { question: 'Why is 1000 Hz refused when the sample rate is 2000 Hz?', answer: 'Half of 2000 Hz is 1000 Hz. This page does not assign a frequency at or above that half. 999 Hz is allowed with that sample rate. 1000 Hz is not started, and it is not written onto an oscillator that is already running. The page uses the sample rate the AudioContext reports. It does not substitute 44100 or 48000.' },
  { question: 'What does a gain position of 30 mean?', answer: 'The slider is an integer from 0 to 100. Linear gain is that integer divided by 100, then multiplied by 0.5. Position 30 is linear gain 0.15. Position 100 is 0.5. Position 0 is 0. Position 1 is 0.005. The gain cell prints that number. It is the GainNode value, not decibels and not a measured loudness.' },
  { question: 'Does Play choose the left or right speaker?', answer: 'No. One oscillator connects through the gain node to the audio context destination. This page has no left-channel control and no right-channel control. The browser and the operating system choose the output device.' },
];
const related = [
  { href: '/microphone-test', title: 'Microphone Test', description: 'Record and play back a short sample. This page does not open the microphone.', icon: '🎤' },
  { href: '/mouse-test', title: 'Mouse Test', description: 'Check buttons, movement, and wheel input.', icon: '◉' },
  { href: '/keyboard-test', title: 'Keyboard Test', description: 'Check keys that reach this browser tab.', icon: '⌨' },
  { href: '/controller-test', title: 'Controller Test', description: 'Read gamepad buttons and axes. This page does not.', icon: '▣' },
  { href: '/reaction-time-test', title: 'Reaction Time Test', description: 'Time a click after a visual cue. No oscillator is started.', icon: '◌' },
];
const schema = [
  { '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'WebApplication', name: 'Adjustable Frequency Tone Generator', url: absoluteUrl('/tone-generator'), applicationCategory: 'UtilitiesApplication', operatingSystem: 'Any', offers: { '@type': 'Offer', price: '0', priceCurrency: 'USD' } },
  { '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'FAQPage', mainEntity: faq.map((item) => ({ '@type': 'Question', name: item.question, acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: item.answer } })) },
];
---

<BaseLayout title="Adjustable Frequency Tone Generator" {description} path="/tone-generator">
  <JsonLd data={schema} />
  <Breadcrumbs items={[{ label: 'Tone Generator' }]} />
  <section class="page-hero shell compact">
    <h1>Adjustable Frequency Tone Generator</h1>
    <p>Choose a frequency and a waveform, then press Play. This browser starts one oscillator.</p>
  </section>
  <ToneGeneratorTool />
  <article class="content">
    <h2>How to use this adjustable frequency tone generator</h2>
    <p>The frequency box starts at 440. That is the initial text in the control, and it is the number Play will send if you do not type a replacement. The box accepts a whole number from 1 through 20000. The waveform choices are sine, square, sawtooth, and triangle. Sine is selected when the page loads. The gain slider starts at position 30. This page converts that position to a linear gain of 0.15. The conversion is the position divided by 100, then multiplied by 0.5. Position 0 is gain 0. Position 100 is gain 0.5. Position 1 is gain 0.005. The page does not set the gain to 1.</p>
    <p>Press Play. The script first checks the frequency, the waveform, and the gain position. If any check fails, it does not construct an AudioContext and it does not create an oscillator. If the checks pass and the browser provides an AudioContext constructor, the script constructs a context, resumes it when the context is suspended, and reads sampleRate. When the frequency is below half of that sample rate, the script creates one OscillatorNode and one GainNode. The oscillator connects to the gain node. The gain node connects to the audio context destination. The oscillator type is the selected waveform. The frequency value is the number in the box, in hertz. The gain value is the linear gain from the slider. The script then calls start on the oscillator.</p>
    <p>Press Stop to call stop on that oscillator and disconnect it. A stopped oscillator cannot be started again. The next Play creates a new oscillator. The AudioContext from the first successful construction stays open, so the sample-rate cell can keep showing the value the context reported. Play is disabled while an oscillator is running, so a second Play does not stack another tone on top of the first. Stop is disabled until an oscillator is running.</p>
    <p>You can change the frequency, the waveform, or the gain while an oscillator is running. A value this page accepts is written to the live node: the frequency to the frequency parameter, the waveform to the type, and the gain to the gain parameter. A value this page rejects is not written. The oscillator keeps the previous frequency, type, and gain. The status line says the new value was not applied, and it repeats the waveform and frequency that are still running. Closing the tab or reloading the page drops the oscillator. This page does not record the tone, does not upload it, and does not keep a log of the frequencies you played.</p>

    <h2>The browser oscillator is an OscillatorNode</h2>
    <p>The running sound is an <a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/webaudio/#OscillatorNode" rel="noopener noreferrer">OscillatorNode</a> from the Web Audio API, heard through whatever output device the browser is already using. The status line calls it a browser oscillator. That is the whole claim about the sound. The page does not test hearing. It does not produce a score. It does not tell you that a frequency is safe, harmful, or the frequency of a ringing sound. It does not match tinnitus. It does not measure frequency response.</p>
    <p>The specification lists the oscillator frequency default as 440 hertz and the type default as sine. This page uses those same values as the initial controls so the first Play has a defined frequency and waveform before you edit them. They are not a measurement of your speakers. The page cannot read the output device name, the operating-system volume, the room, or a microphone. The <a href="/microphone-test">Microphone Test</a> records an input and plays that recording back. Play on this page does not call getUserMedia, and the microphone page does not create an OscillatorNode.</p>
    <p>This page never writes the detune parameter. A new oscillator keeps the detune value the browser supplies. The Web Audio specification sets that default to 0 cents. This page does not add a pitch offset of its own, and it does not display cents. The applied-frequency cell is the hertz value written to the frequency parameter, not a pitch estimated from a recording.</p>

    <h2>Frequency is the number assigned to the oscillator</h2>
    <p>Type a whole number into the frequency box, or use the stepper the browser draws for a number input. The text must be digits only, from 1 to 20000, after trimming spaces at the ends. 440 is accepted. 1 and 20000 are accepted. 0, 20001, a negative sign, a decimal point, a blank box, a word, and a number written with an exponent are not accepted. The page does not round 439.6 to 440, and it does not clamp 20001 down to 20000. Both are rejected so the status line can say the oscillator was not changed. A rejected Play does not construct an AudioContext.</p>
    <p>The applied-frequency cell shows the last value this page accepted. If the box contains 0 while an oscillator is running at 880 Hz, the cell stays on 880 Hz. The oscillator frequency parameter is left at 880. The status line says the typed value was not changed and that the oscillator is still playing at 880 Hz. Clearing the box has the same result. The page does not treat a blank box as zero hertz, because a written zero would look like a frequency this control does not allow.</p>
    <p>440 means 440 cycles per second on the oscillator frequency parameter. It does not mean the air in the room was measured at 440 Hz. Playing 1000 Hz and then 2000 Hz, when both numbers are allowed, only changes that parameter. The page does not store a level, a grade, or a curve for either number. There is no sweep.</p>

    <h2>Half the AudioContext sample rate</h2>
    <p>After an AudioContext exists, the sample-rate cell shows context.sampleRate in hertz. This page does not assign a frequency greater than or equal to half of that rate. The refusal is a rule of this page. It is not a hearing result, and it is not a measurement of a speaker. A context that reports 48000 Hz has a half of 24000 Hz. The frequency box already stops at 20000, so that context accepts every whole number the box accepts, including 20000. A context that reports a lower rate can refuse a number the box would otherwise allow.</p>
    <p>A context sample rate of 2000 Hz has a half of 1000 Hz. On that context, 1000 Hz is refused and 999 Hz is allowed. The refused Play creates the context far enough to read the sample rate, then does not call start. If an oscillator is already running, a later 1000 Hz entry is not written onto it. The status line quotes the sample rate the context actually reported. This page does not replace a missing, zero, negative, or non-finite sample rate with 44100 or 48000. When the sample rate cannot be used, the oscillator is not started.</p>
    <p>The sample-rate cell reads Not started until the first Play has created a context. If the browser has no AudioContext constructor, the status line says this browser did not provide AudioContext, and no oscillator is started. If the constructor or resume throws, the status line says the browser did not start or did not resume the AudioContext. This page does not fall back to an audio file, an HTML audio element, or a tone generated by another method. If start throws after the nodes were created, the script disconnects those nodes and leaves the oscillator cell off. It does not claim a tone is playing.</p>

    <h2>Waveform is one of four OscillatorNode types</h2>
    <p>The waveform control is a set of radio buttons. The values are the four type names this page sets on the oscillator: sine, square, sawtooth, and triangle. The Web Audio API also allows a custom periodic wave. This page does not build one. If the selected value is not one of those four names, Play does not start an oscillator, and a running oscillator keeps its previous type.</p>
    <p>Sine, square, sawtooth, and triangle are the built-in shapes. This page does not add harmonics of its own, and it does not describe a shape as brighter, warmer, or louder. Those words are listening impressions. The only waveform change this page makes is the type property. The waveform cell prints the type string that was written, in the same lowercase spelling as the control value. Changing the radio while an oscillator is running updates that type immediately when the value is one of the four names.</p>

    <h2>Gain is the GainNode value, not a measured loudness</h2>
    <p>The slider is an integer from 0 to 100. Linear gain equals that integer divided by 100, multiplied by 0.5. The gain cell prints the result. 30 becomes 0.15. 100 becomes 0.5. 0 becomes 0. 1 becomes 0.005. 2 becomes 0.01. The printed gain is the number this page assigns to the GainNode. An AudioParam may store that assignment in 32-bit floating point, so reading the parameter back can differ in a later decimal place. The cell keeps the formula's decimal. It is not decibels, not sound pressure, and not a percentage of the volume you hear. The operating-system volume and the selected output device sit past the audio context destination. This page does not read those controls.</p>
    <p>Gain 0 can still start an oscillator. You may hear nothing because the gain node multiplies the oscillator by zero. That silence is the gain value. It is not a verdict that a speaker is unplugged, and it is not a frequency-response point. The default of 0.15 is only the starting position, so the first Play is not gain 1. It is not a safe-listening limit, and it is not advice about how long to listen. System volume still applies. Stop ends the oscillator.</p>
    <p>The gain is written before the oscillator is connected and started, so the first samples use the selected gain rather than the GainNode default of 1. Moving the slider while a tone is running replaces the gain parameter with the new linear value. A slider position outside 0 to 100 is rejected. The range control on this page already stops at those ends. A typed or scripted value such as 101 or a blank position is not applied.</p>

    <h2>What this page does not conclude</h2>
    <p>Nothing on the page is a hearing test. Nothing on the page matches tinnitus to a frequency. Nothing on the page measures frequency response. A tone that seems quiet at a high number is not a curve this page calculated. The page has no loopback and no level meter. It cannot tell you that the output matches the number in the box after the signal leaves the audio context. Quote the status line if you need a record of what was set. A playing line includes the waveform, the frequency in hertz, the linear gain, and the AudioContext sample rate. It does not include a grade.</p>
    <p>The page does not choose a left output or a right output. One oscillator is connected, through the gain node, to the destination. There is no balance slider. A hardware controller can include a speaker. This page does not open the Gamepad API and does not address a controller speaker. The <a href="/controller-test">Controller Test</a> reads buttons and axes. The <a href="/mouse-test">Mouse Test</a>, the <a href="/keyboard-test">Keyboard Test</a>, and the <a href="/reaction-time-test">Reaction Time Test</a> read clicks, keys, or a timed visual cue. None of them start an oscillator. Use those pages for input. Use this page when you want a generated waveform at a frequency you type.</p>
    <p>Run Play again after Stop when you want a new oscillator. You can also change the number while the current oscillator is running, and a number this page accepts is applied immediately. Two Plays in a row are not two stacked tones: the Play button stays off until Stop. Search interest for this tool is not stated here, because this page has no verified search figures. The sample-rate cell and the status line are browser values from the session you start. They are not a ranking of headphones, speakers, or computers.</p>
    <NewToolRelatedTests tests={related} />
    <FAQ items={faq} />
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
